# Copy to .env at the repo root — the bridge (dotenv) and vite (envDir: '..')
# both read this single file. Real environment variables override these.

# --- Clerk (Phases 3-4: auth gate + per-user identity) ---
# Web sign-in UI. From Clerk dashboard → API keys.
VITE_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
# Bridge-side token verification. Keep secret.
CLERK_SECRET_KEY=

# Force-disable the auth gate even with keys set (local single-user mode).
# The gate is also off automatically whenever CLERK_SECRET_KEY is empty.
#BRIDGE_AUTH_DISABLED=1

# Adopting an existing ~/.lines-app install (or a second machine's copy) into
# your user is a script — append-safe, run once per source env:
#   npm run migrate -w server -- <clerk-user-id> [--from <dir>]

# --- Supabase / storage server (Phase 5) ---
# Supabase dashboard → Settings → Database. Pooled (pgbouncer) string for the app:
#DATABASE_URL=
# Direct string for `prisma migrate`:
#DIRECT_URL=
# PORT belongs to the storage server alone (storage/src/index.ts, default 8790).
# The bridge and worker no longer read it — see the local-port section below.
# Where the bridge finds the storage server.
#STORAGE_URL=http://localhost:8790
# Where the *browser* finds it. Separate from STORAGE_URL because this one is
# baked into the web bundle at build time, and it is what switches on machine
# pairing and session sharing in the UI (both need somewhere to keep a grant).
# Unset, the app talks only to a local bridge and neither surface appears.
#VITE_STORAGE_URL=http://localhost:8790
# CORS allowlist for browser->storage calls, comma-separated exact origins.
# Required as soon as VITE_STORAGE_URL is set: the browser then calls storage
# cross-origin, and without a match here storage sends no
# Access-Control-Allow-Origin and every call fails as an opaque "Failed to
# fetch". Not a wildcard on purpose — these responses carry user data.
#WEB_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173

# --- Local ports ---
# Both local processes bind an ephemeral port by default and publish it to
# ~/.lines-app/run/<instance>/{worker,bridge}.json, so two Lines installs on one
# machine can never fight over a port. Set these only to pin one deliberately
# (Tilt does, so its readiness probes have a fixed target).
#LINES_BRIDGE_PORT=8787
#LINES_WORKER_PORT=8788
# Separates concurrent installs' discovery files. The desktop app sets `desktop`;
# a dev checkout (Tilt included) leaves it unset, so it is `default`. Tilt sets
# nothing deliberately: vite resolves /__bridge from this same variable, so pinning
# it for the bridge alone would stop the dev server finding it.
#LINES_INSTANCE=default
# One bridge per machine: the second one to start refuses and exits 78, because
# ~/.lines-app assumes a sole writer (the lock is ~/.lines-app/bridge.lock, and it
# is claimed whether or not the bridge relays). The exception is the tray app's
# bridge, which a dev bridge preempts — it stands down and re-arms itself. Set this
# to run two bridges over one ~/.lines-app anyway; transcript corruption is then
# yours to own.
#LINES_ALLOW_MULTIPLE_BRIDGES=1

# --- Hosted deployment (behind a reverse proxy) ---
# Where the browser should open the bridge socket. Read at BUILD time only
# (web/src/ws.ts), so changing it needs a rebuild, not a restart.
# Unset — the dev default — the web client probes /__bridge, a middleware that
# exists only in the vite dev server: in a static build the SPA fallback answers
# with index.html, JSON parsing throws, and the UI reconnects forever.
# Must be wss:// on an HTTPS page, and must NOT end in a slash: ws.ts appends
# "/?token=<jwt>" itself.
# Pin LINES_BRIDGE_PORT above alongside this — a proxy needs a fixed target,
# and the ephemeral default moves on every restart.
#VITE_BRIDGE_WS_URL=wss://lines.example.com/bridge
# Direct link to the published macOS DMG. Read at BUILD time only. Unset, the web
# app shows no download surface at all — the pairing screens then assume the
# desktop app is already installed, which is only true in a dev checkout.
# Point it at the stable alias key, not a versioned filename: `npm run ship -w
# desktop` republishes that key on every release, so this is set once and a
# desktop release never touches the web build again.
#VITE_DESKTOP_DOWNLOAD_URL=https://pub-xxxx.r2.dev/desktop/Lines-latest.dmg
# Full VPS runbook and a production .env template: deploy/README.md and
# deploy/env.production.example.

# --- Relay (hosted access) ---
# Unset by default: the bridge only dials out when RELAY_URL is set, so a purely
# local setup never touches the relay. `tilt up -- --with-relay` wires all three.
#RELAY_URL=ws://127.0.0.1:8791
#LINES_DEVICE_ID=
#LINES_DEVICE_SECRET=
# How long the bridge tolerates silence on the relay link before terminating its
# own socket and re-dialling. The relay pings every RELAY_AGENT_PING_MS below, so
# a healthy but idle link still delivers frames well inside this. Lower it only to
# test the watchdog: a half-open socket is what this detects.
#LINES_RELAY_IDLE_MS=60000
# How long a relay socket must survive before it counts as a working link. Only a
# socket that lasts this long resets the reconnect backoff — an `open` alone does
# not, because the relay accepts a dial it is about to supersede.
#LINES_RELAY_STABLE_MS=10000
# After this many consecutive supersedes (close 1012) with no stable link in
# between, the bridge treats it as two processes claiming one device identity and
# raises its re-dial cap to LINES_SUPERSEDE_CAP_MS instead of hammering the relay.
# Stopping the other bridge recovers on the next dial.
#LINES_SUPERSEDE_LIMIT=5
#LINES_SUPERSEDE_CAP_MS=300000
# Relay server itself:
#RELAY_PORT=8791
# Keepalive on the /agent link. The relay pings an attached bridge this often and
# terminates one that has been silent for RELAY_AGENT_DEAD_MS (~2 missed pings).
# Without it a half-open socket leaves the device looking online forever and the
# browser spinning. Tests compress these; a deployment should not need to.
#RELAY_AGENT_PING_MS=20000
#RELAY_AGENT_DEAD_MS=55000
# How often an already-attached device's claim is re-checked against storage, so a
# revoke stops it serving within this window rather than only at its next
# reconnect. One /v1/devices/verify per attached device per interval. It also
# refreshes lastSeenAt, which is what keeps a machine's relay-reported liveness
# believable — see DEVICE_PRESENCE_TTL_MS below.
#RELAY_REVERIFY_MS=300000
# Storage: how long a machine's `online` flag is believed after its last contact.
# The relay writes that flag on hub attach/detach, so a relay crash would leave
# every machine reading online forever; past this window storage reports offline
# and the web app falls back to "last seen". Two re-verify periods by default.
#DEVICE_PRESENCE_TTL_MS=600000
# Dev-only. Accepts any device secret and binds every client to one user — never
# set this in a deployment.
#RELAY_AUTH_DISABLED=1

# --- Cloudflare R2 (recipe images) ---
# Optional: recipes work without images — unset, the storage server warns once
# and the upload route answers 503 while the recipe still saves.
# The bucket must be public-read (r2.dev subdomain or a custom domain): stored
# URLs are handed to the browser as-is and are never signed.
#R2_ACCOUNT_ID=
#R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
#R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
#R2_BUCKET=
# Public base the object keys are appended to, e.g. https://pub-xxxx.r2.dev or a
# custom domain. NOT the S3 API endpoint (<account>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com):
# a browser fetch there is unsigned, so uploads would appear to work and every
# stored image would silently render broken. The storage server warns at boot.
#R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=

# --- Cloudflare R2, desktop releases (a SECOND bucket) ---
# `npm run release -w desktop` uploads the DMG, the zip and latest-mac.yml here.
# Deliberately not R2_BUCKET above: public-read is a bucket-level setting, so
# sharing one would make every user-uploaded recipe screenshot world-readable in
# order to publish an installer. Everything in this bucket is meant to be public.
# The API token in R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID must be scoped to both buckets (or be
# account-level), otherwise the upload 403s.
#R2_RELEASE_BUCKET=
# Same rule as R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL: the public r2.dev subdomain or a custom domain,
# never the S3 API endpoint. The release script refuses to upload otherwise —
# electron-updater and the browser both fetch these unsigned.
#R2_RELEASE_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=
